    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d181-a1">

                <pb facs="0263" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg195"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Editor of the “Milwaukee Journal”</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">L. W. Nieman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to President
                        Wilson </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Milwaukee, Wis.</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1915-01-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 26, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: I forgot to ask you for
                    a companion statement from the State department, dealing with the complaints of
                    friends of the allies. This would greatly strengthen the answer to the partisans
                    of Germany. In no other way could the difficulties of our government be so
                    strikingly brought home to the crowd on both sides. To be useful, this statement
                    should come soon and should not be confined to the case of Belgium, but should
                    also take up minor matters. I went somewhat into details with Mr. McAdoo and Mr.
                    Tumulty.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">L. W. Nieman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
